<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dutch West India Company</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1621">1621</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Geoctroyeerde Westindische Compagnie, GWC</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> Company was a chartered company established in 1621 to conduct Dutch trade and warfare in the Atlantic. It played a central role in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-empire">Dutch Empire</a>'s activities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a>, though it struggled financially and was later reorganised.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dutch-west-india-company.jpg" alt="Dutch West India Company" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zscout370; derivative work by Fentener van Vlissingen; derivative work by Mnmazur · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Dutch_West_India_Company.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>During the Iberian Union Dutch privateers sacked Salvador in 1604 and took it in May 1624 for eleven months; from 1630 to 1654 the <b>Dutch West India Company</b> held Recife, Olinda and, after Paraíba fell in 1635, the sugar coast as Dutch Brazil until it withdrew in 1654.</blockquote><a href="/empire/colonial-brazil">Colonial Brazil · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Geoctroyeerde Westindische Compagnie</b> (GWC) followed in 1621 to extend Dutch reach into the Atlantic, the Americas and West Africa.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-empire">Dutch Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1628 Piet Heyn of the <b>Dutch West India Company</b> captured an entire Spanish silver fleet off Matanzas in Cuba, a haul that paid the company’s shareholders a large dividend.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>When the <b>Dutch West India Company</b> seized Luanda in August 1641 she allied with the Dutch and recovered much of Ndongo, defeating the Portuguese at Ngoleme in 1644 and at Kombi in 1647, although she lost a battle at Kavanga in 1646 in which her sister Kambu was…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-ndongo">Kingdom of Ndongo · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>On 3 June 1621 the Republic chartered the <b>Dutch West India Company</b> with exclusive rights in West Africa and the Americas.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-netherland">New Netherland · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Zeeland struggled to run the colony and sold it in 1682 to the <b>Dutch West India Company</b>, which in 1683 formed the chartered Society of Suriname with the city of Amsterdam and Cornelis van Aerssen van Sommelsdijck, each holding one third.</blockquote><a href="/empire/suriname">Suriname · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
